        "content": "                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n        1. What  is HTML?                                                         \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n        HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard language used to create and\n        structure content on the web. It's not a programming language — it's a markup\n        language that uses tags to describe the structure and meaning of content (headings,\n                                                                                  \n        paragraphs, links, images, etc.) so browsers know how to display it.      \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n        2. What  is a tag?                                                        \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n        A tag is a keyword enclosed in angle brackets that tells the browser how to interpret\n                                                                                  \n        content. Tags usually come in pairs:                                      \n                                                                                  \n        •Opening tag: <p>                                                         \n        •Closing tag: </p> (has a forward slash)                                  \n                                                                                  \n        3. Tag vs Element                                                         \n                                                                                  \n        Aspect       Tag                      Element                             \n                                                                                  \n                     The markup keyword enclosed in The complete unit: opening tag +\n        Definition                                                                \n                     angle brackets           content + closing tag               \n                                                                                  \n        Example      <p> and </p>             <p>Hello</p>                        \n                                                                                  \n        Contains                              Yes — includes the content between  \n                     No — just the markup itself                                  \n        content?                              tags                                \n        Count                                                                     \n                     2 tags (one opening, one closing) 1 element                  \n        in <p>Hello</p>                                                           \n        Void tags    <img>, <br>, <input> (no closing tag)Same as the tag (no content)\n                                                                                  \n        Attributes   Held on the tag, e.g. <a href=\"...\"> Part of the element     \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n        4. <head> vs <body>                                                       \n                                                                                  \n        Aspect         <head>                   <body>                            \n                                                                                  \n        Visibility     ❌  Not visible to the user ✅ Visible to the user           \n                                                                                  \n                       Metadata and setup about the                               \n        Purpose                                 The actual rendered content       \n                       page                                                       \n                       <title>, <meta>, <link>, <style>, <script                  \n        Typical contents                        <h1>, <p>, <img>, <a>, <div>, <form>\n                       >                                                          \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  \n                                                                                  ",
